<%# How the app works, briefly: what a scan does, how the rules rate, how to read the
    result, and a link to the longer write-up. The rating levels are generated from
    the rule engine's own data (HelpController), so they cannot drift from the rules. %>
<% content_for :title, "How it works" %>
<% link_class = "text-brand-700 hover:text-brand-800 dark:text-brand-300 dark:hover:text-brand-200 underline rounded focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-brand-500" %>
<% heading_class = "text-lg font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8 mb-3" %>
<div class="min-h-screen flex flex-col bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-900">
  <div class="max-w-3xl w-full mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8 flex-grow">
    <%= render "shared/header" %>

    <article class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-lg p-6 sm:p-8 mb-6 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200 mb-3">How CRXaminer works</h1>
      <p>
        CRXaminer shows what a Chrome extension is able to do before you install it.
        It reads the extension's package and its Chrome Web Store listing. It never runs the extension.
      </p>

      <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">What a scan does</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal ml-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200">Reads the store listing:</strong> name, developer, users, rating and last update.</li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200">Downloads the package</strong> from Google and reads its
          <code class="font-mono text-sm">manifest.json</code>, the file where an extension declares what it needs.
          It also lists the URLs that appear in the extension's files.
        </li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200">Applies the rules</strong> below to the manifest.</li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200">Asks an AI model for a verdict in context.</strong>
          The model (Claude) sees the listing, the manifest and the rule findings, and weighs what the extension asks for against what it says it does.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-3">
        Scans are saved, so everyone sees the same result. A scan older than 24 hours can be run again from its page.
      </p>

      <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>" id="rules">The rule engine</h2>
      <p>
        The rules read only the manifest. They rate what the extension is allowed to do, assuming the worst case, not what its code actually does.
        Each finding has a level, and the rule-based rating is the highest level found. Nothing is added up.
      </p>

      <%= render "levels" %>

      <p class="mt-3">
        Optional permissions are rated one level lower, because Chrome asks you before granting them.
        On a scan page, the Manifest tab shows the line behind each finding.
      </p>

      <h2 class="<%= heading_class %>">Reading the result</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc ml-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200">Context-aware verdict:</strong> the AI model's rating. It can be wrong, so treat it as a second opinion.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-200">Rule-based rating and findings:</strong> always the same for the same manifest.</li>
        <li>A low rating does not prove an extension is safe. CRXaminer does not review the extension's code.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-8 pt-4 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">
        For the full story, read
        <%= link_to "the CRXaminer write-up on Astarte", HelpController::WRITE_UP_URL, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: link_class %>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <%= render "shared/footer" %>
  </div>
</div>
